6. DATA INTEGRITY AND ANALYTICAL ACCEPTANCE PROGRAM

The control center must be tested against the real characteristics of real-estate data.

6.1 Entity Resolution

Test:

* Same property with different addresses.
* Different properties with similar addresses.
* Same building with multiple units.
* Duplicate listings for one property.
* Reposted listings.
* Changed ownership claims.
* Missing geographic coordinates.
* Inconsistent area measurements.
* Ambiguous property types.

Measure:

* False merges.
* Missed duplicates.
* Precision.
* Recall.
* Manual review rate.
* Unresolved ambiguity.

Do not optimize duplicate detection solely for higher automation.

A false merge may be more damaging than leaving two records temporarily separate.

6.2 Data Quality

Test:

* Missing values.
* Invalid values.
* Contradictory values.
* Stale values.
* Out-of-range values.
* Source changes.
* Schema changes.
* Unexpected distribution changes.

Every missing value must have a defined semantic state.

Do not confuse:

* Zero.
* Unknown.
* Not collected.
* Not applicable.
* Not available.
* Expired.
* Invalid.
* Withheld.
* Suppressed.

6.3 Historical Reconstruction

Select representative records and reconstruct:

* What was known at a past time.
* Which source supplied each value.
* Which transformations occurred.
* Which value was considered valid.
* Which analytical outputs depended on it.

The system must not claim historical reconstruction if required history was overwritten.

6.4 Analytical Reproducibility

For selected reports and valuations, reproduce the original result using:

* Original data version.
* Original model version.
* Original parameters.
* Original geographic definitions.
* Original time window.
* Original source set.

Record any differences.

Distinguish exact reproducibility from approximate reproducibility.

6.5 Valuation Validation

Test valuation behavior across:

* Neighborhoods.
* Property types.
* Building ages.
* Areas.
* Price ranges.
* New and old listings.
* High-density and low-density regions.
* Different market conditions.

Evaluate:

* Prediction error.
* Error distribution.
* Confidence intervals.
* Sample quality.
* Sensitivity to outliers.
* Sensitivity to source composition.
* Model drift.
* Geographic bias.

A single average error metric is not sufficient.

A model can have a good overall average and still perform very poorly in a particular neighborhood or property category.

6.6 Publication Reliability

Before publishing a market indicator, verify:

* Data coverage.
* Source independence.
* Sample adequacy.
* Geographic consistency.
* Temporal consistency.
* Methodological stability.
* Known limitations.
* Uncertainty.
* Reproducibility.

Support publication suppression when evidence is insufficient.

⸻

7. MONITORING AND OBSERVABILITY ACCEPTANCE PROGRAM

The monitoring system must prove that its measurements are reliable enough to support decisions.

7.1 Metric Accuracy

For every critical metric, verify:

* Definition.
* Unit.
* Source.
* Collection method.
* Sampling interval.
* Aggregation method.
* Timestamp basis.
* Freshness.
* Missing-data behavior.
* Known limitations.

Compare selected metrics against independent measurements where possible.

7.2 CPU and Memory

Verify that available CPU and memory measurements are accurate.

Test:

* Sustained CPU load.
* Sudden CPU spikes.
* Memory growth.
* Memory leaks.
* Garbage collection pressure.
* Process restarts.
* Memory limits.
* Out-of-memory behavior.

For serverless services, display provider-supported measurements only.

If CPU or RAM usage cannot be measured, display NOT AVAILABLE or NOT EXPOSED.

Never display an invented zero.

7.3 Storage

Test:

* Capacity growth.
* Storage exhaustion.
* Object count growth.
* Failed uploads.
* Incomplete multipart uploads.
* Orphan files.
* Retention execution.
* Backup growth.
* Database storage growth.

Verify that capacity forecasts use actual historical measurements.

7.4 Network and API

